**`Array.prototype.map` polyfill.** It must validate the callback, honour `thisArg`, pass `(value, index, array)`, skip holes in sparse arrays, and return a new array.
```js
Array.prototype.myMap = function (cb, thisArg) {
  if (typeof cb !== 'function') throw new TypeError(cb + ' is not a function');
  const out = new Array(this.length);
  for (let i = 0; i < this.length; i++) {
    if (i in this) out[i] = cb.call(thisArg, this[i], i, this);
  }
  return out;
};
```

**Debounce:** delays execution until `wait` ms have passed since the **last** call. Use it for search-as-you-type or window resize end.
```js
function debounce(fn, wait) {
  let t;
  return function (...args) {
    clearTimeout(t);
    t = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), wait);
  };
}
```

**Throttle:** allows at most one execution per `wait` ms, however often it is called. Use it for scroll, mousemove or button spam.
```js
function throttle(fn, wait) {
  let last = 0;
  return function (...args) {
    const now = Date.now();
    if (now - last >= wait) { last = now; fn.apply(this, args); }
  };
}
```
This is the leading-edge version; mention that a trailing call and `cancel` can be added. Both rely on **closures** to keep `t` or `last`, and use `apply` to preserve `this` and arguments.

